{
  "version": 1,
  "courseId": 1362070,
  "sourceLanguage": "en",
  "targetLanguage": "zh-TW",
  "lectures": [
    {
      "id": "25599698",
      "title": "Working with State & Events",
      "sourceHash": "cfd50c706511353269e7644e8e336a198b381fab429f1e7fc2284be9f97cc083",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "start": 2.1,
          "end": 5.74,
          "en": "So let's now convert this Users component",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "start": 5.74,
          "end": 8.42,
          "en": "to a class-based component.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "start": 8.42,
          "end": 12.66,
          "en": "For this, we again should import something from react.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "start": 12.66,
          "end": 14.85,
          "en": "Let me restructure these imports.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "start": 14.85,
          "end": 19.31,
          "en": "We should import Component from react",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "start": 20.38,
          "end": 23.6,
          "en": "because we now build our users component",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "start": 23.6,
          "end": 28.32,
          "en": "by creating a class named Users which extends Component.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "start": 28.32,
          "end": 30.63,
          "en": "That's what I explained in the last lecture",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "start": 30.63,
          "end": 34.713,
          "en": "and that's how you always build your class-based components.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "start": 35.89,
          "end": 38.73,
          "en": "In there we can now add a render method",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "start": 38.73,
          "end": 42.62,
          "en": "and in that render method we return to jsx code",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 12,
          "start": 42.62,
          "end": 44.26,
          "en": "that should be rendered.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 13,
          "start": 44.26,
          "end": 46.41,
          "en": "So, I'll copy this return statement",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 14,
          "start": 46.41,
          "end": 48.253,
          "en": "and bring it into my render method.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 15,
          "start": 49.67,
          "end": 54.01,
          "en": "Now we learned that we can access props with these props",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 16,
          "start": 54.01,
          "end": 56.33,
          "en": "but here I'm not working with props",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 17,
          "start": 56.33,
          "end": 58.96,
          "en": "instead, I'm relying on state",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 18,
          "start": 58.96,
          "end": 61.48,
          "en": "and I am pointing at a function",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 19,
          "start": 61.48,
          "end": 64.03,
          "en": "that should be executed when the button is clicked.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 20,
          "start": 65.29,
          "end": 68.67,
          "en": "Now let's start by adding that function.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 21,
          "start": 68.67,
          "end": 70.59,
          "en": "In class-based components,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 22,
          "start": 70.59,
          "end": 75.03,
          "en": "you now don't add a function inside of the render method",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 23,
          "start": 75.03,
          "end": 77.34,
          "en": "though that would technically be possible",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 24,
          "start": 77.34,
          "end": 79.96000000000001,
          "en": "but it wouldn't behave correctly.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 25,
          "start": 79.96000000000001,
          "end": 82.3,
          "en": "But instead, since this is a class,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 26,
          "start": 82.3,
          "end": 84.93,
          "en": "you group together functionality",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 27,
          "start": 84.93,
          "end": 87.51,
          "en": "by grouping it all in that class.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 28,
          "start": 87.51,
          "end": 90.33,
          "en": "So you would simply add a method",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 29,
          "start": 90.33,
          "end": 93.483,
          "en": "named toggleUsersHandler like this.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 30,
          "start": 94.5,
          "end": 96.52000000000001,
          "en": "There are also alternative ways",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 31,
          "start": 96.52000000000001,
          "end": 98.78999999999999,
          "en": "of writing a method in classes",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 32,
          "start": 98.78999999999999,
          "end": 101.68,
          "en": "but this is the most default and standard way",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 33,
          "start": 101.68,
          "end": 103.213,
          "en": "of defining a method.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 34,
          "start": 105.33,
          "end": 108.6,
          "en": "Now we have the method, but we don't have the state.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 35,
          "start": 108.6,
          "end": 112.86,
          "en": "And managing state worked totally different",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 36,
          "start": 112.86,
          "end": 116.22999999999999,
          "en": "in the past with class-based components.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 37,
          "start": 116.22999999999999,
          "end": 120.2,
          "en": "With react16.8 hooks were introduced",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 38,
          "start": 120.2,
          "end": 122.5,
          "en": "and we could use to UseState hook",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 39,
          "start": 122.5,
          "end": 125.54,
          "en": "to manage state and functional components.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 40,
          "start": 125.54,
          "end": 128.453,
          "en": "And it is very simple to manage state then.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 41,
          "start": 130.03,
          "end": 133.52,
          "en": "Prior to 16.8 class-based components",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 42,
          "start": 133.52,
          "end": 137.1,
          "en": "were the only kind of component that could manage state.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 43,
          "start": 137.1,
          "end": 140.12,
          "en": "So you always had to write class-based components",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 44,
          "start": 140.12,
          "end": 142.28,
          "en": "when you needed state.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 45,
          "start": 142.28,
          "end": 146.71,
          "en": "And there you basically needed to do two things,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 46,
          "start": 146.71,
          "end": 149.52,
          "en": "initialize and define your state,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 47,
          "start": 149.52,
          "end": 153.65,
          "en": "like we're doing it with UseState when we first call it",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 48,
          "start": 153.65,
          "end": 155.95,
          "en": "and then updated when needed,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 49,
          "start": 155.95,
          "end": 159.81,
          "en": "like we're doing it here in the toggleUsersHandler.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 50,
          "start": 159.81,
          "end": 162.87,
          "en": "Now to define state and class-based components,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 51,
          "start": 162.87,
          "end": 165.18,
          "en": "you would use the constructor.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 52,
          "start": 165.18,
          "end": 168.2,
          "en": "The constructor function is automatically called",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 53,
          "start": 168.2,
          "end": 171.09,
          "en": "whenever this clause is instantiated",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 54,
          "start": 171.09,
          "end": 174.07,
          "en": "which happens when react encounters,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 55,
          "start": 174.07,
          "end": 177.023,
          "en": "your class being used as a component.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 56,
          "start": 178.03,
          "end": 181.6,
          "en": "And then in the constructor, you can do initialization work",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 57,
          "start": 181.6,
          "end": 183.61,
          "en": "like initializing state.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 58,
          "start": 183.61,
          "end": 188.28,
          "en": "And this would be done by accessing this.state",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 59,
          "start": 188.28,
          "end": 190.77,
          "en": "and setting it equal to an object.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 60,
          "start": 190.77,
          "end": 193.64,
          "en": "And this is super crucial now.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 61,
          "start": 193.64,
          "end": 198.64,
          "en": "With class-based components, your state always important,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 62,
          "start": 200.15,
          "end": 203.24,
          "en": "always is an object.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 63,
          "start": 203.24,
          "end": 207.41,
          "en": "With functional components, your state can be anything.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 64,
          "start": 207.41,
          "end": 211.21,
          "en": "It can be just a Boolean, just a string, just a number",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 65,
          "start": 211.21,
          "end": 214.21,
          "en": "but it can also be an object, but it's flexible",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 66,
          "start": 214.21,
          "end": 215.653,
          "en": "it can be anything.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 67,
          "start": 216.63,
          "end": 219.49,
          "en": "With class-based components, that's not the case.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 68,
          "start": 219.49,
          "end": 222.12,
          "en": "Here your state always is an object",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 69,
          "start": 223.44,
          "end": 225.97,
          "en": "because with class-based components,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 70,
          "start": 225.97,
          "end": 230.44,
          "en": "you always group all the state's license and pieces",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 71,
          "start": 230.44,
          "end": 234.51,
          "en": "you might need in a component into one state object.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 72,
          "start": 234.51,
          "end": 236.52,
          "en": "With functional components",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 73,
          "start": 236.52,
          "end": 239.52,
          "en": "instead if you have multiple state pieces,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 74,
          "start": 239.52,
          "end": 242.403,
          "en": "you could use multiple UseState calls.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 75,
          "start": 243.45,
          "end": 246.77,
          "en": "You could also create just one state object",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 76,
          "start": 246.77,
          "end": 248.47,
          "en": "and group it together there",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 77,
          "start": 248.47,
          "end": 252.04,
          "en": "but that is optional with functional components.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 78,
          "start": 252.04,
          "end": 255.893,
          "en": "And I do talk about that in the state course section.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 79,
          "start": 256.92,
          "end": 259.87,
          "en": "With class-based components, this is not optional",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 80,
          "start": 259.87,
          "end": 263.05,
          "en": "there you have to group all the state",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 81,
          "start": 263.05,
          "end": 265.71,
          "en": "that makes up a component into one object.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 82,
          "start": 265.71,
          "end": 270.053,
          "en": "No matter if these state pieces are closely related or not.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 83,
          "start": 271.04,
          "end": 274.55,
          "en": "So here we would then have our showUsers state",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 84,
          "start": 274.55,
          "end": 277.64,
          "en": "and set this to true initially let's say.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 85,
          "start": 277.64,
          "end": 279.403,
          "en": "And if we had moreState",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 86,
          "start": 280.45,
          "end": 283.493,
          "en": "then we would simply add this as a extra property.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 87,
          "start": 284.42,
          "end": 286.69,
          "en": "You can of course also have nestedState,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 88,
          "start": 286.69,
          "end": 288.5,
          "en": "you could have a nestedObject.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 89,
          "start": 288.5,
          "end": 293.01,
          "en": "You can have a eraseState that all works",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 90,
          "start": 293.01,
          "end": 296.383,
          "en": "but you have one state object which groups it all together.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 91,
          "start": 298.21,
          "end": 300.43,
          "en": "Then if you wanna change your state",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 92,
          "start": 300.43,
          "end": 302.78,
          "en": "like in toggleUsersHandler,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 93,
          "start": 302.78,
          "end": 306.55,
          "en": "you don't do this by accessing this state",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 94,
          "start": 306.55,
          "end": 311.113,
          "en": "and changing it like this, that's not how you do it.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 95,
          "start": 312.58,
          "end": 317.58,
          "en": "Instead, you call a special method, setState, this.setState,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 96,
          "start": 320.46,
          "end": 322.58,
          "en": "and this method on this class",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 97,
          "start": 322.58,
          "end": 325.8,
          "en": "is of course also provided by the component class",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 98,
          "start": 325.8,
          "end": 327.363,
          "en": "we are inheriting from.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 99,
          "start": 328.72,
          "end": 333.72,
          "en": "This setState then also always takes an object.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 100,
          "start": 334.75,
          "end": 339.75,
          "en": "And this object now contains the new state, you wanna set,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 101,
          "start": 339.82,
          "end": 344.24,
          "en": "but very important, it won't override the old state",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 102,
          "start": 344.24,
          "end": 347.24,
          "en": "but instead react will behind the scenes",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 103,
          "start": 347.24,
          "end": 350.13,
          "en": "merge the object you're passing here",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 104,
          "start": 350.13,
          "end": 351.733,
          "en": "with your existing state.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 105,
          "start": 352.76,
          "end": 357.12,
          "en": "So if I had another state here, which I don't need here",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 106,
          "start": 357.12,
          "end": 362.12,
          "en": "if I had that and I then would set showUsers to false,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 107,
          "start": 362.42,
          "end": 364.77,
          "en": "showUsers would be set to false",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 108,
          "start": 364.77,
          "end": 366.92,
          "en": "but the old state would be capped.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 109,
          "start": 366.92,
          "end": 368.68,
          "en": "The other state would be capped",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 110,
          "start": 368.68,
          "end": 372.8,
          "en": "because react merges this update with the existing state",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 111,
          "start": 372.8,
          "end": 375.42,
          "en": "it doesn't override the state.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 112,
          "start": 375.42,
          "end": 378.19,
          "en": "And that's different with useState,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 113,
          "start": 378.19,
          "end": 381.08,
          "en": "here when you call the state updating function",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 114,
          "start": 381.08,
          "end": 384.07,
          "en": "you always override the old state",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 115,
          "start": 384.07,
          "end": 387.343,
          "en": "with whatever you pass to the updating function,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 116,
          "start": 388.25,
          "end": 390.21,
          "en": "unless it is a function in which case",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 117,
          "start": 390.21,
          "end": 393.05,
          "en": "we executes that to derive the new state.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 118,
          "start": 393.05,
          "end": 395.29,
          "en": "But you override the old state,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 119,
          "start": 395.29,
          "end": 397.90999999999997,
          "en": "it's not merged automatically by react",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 120,
          "start": 397.90999999999997,
          "end": 399.74,
          "en": "if you would want merging",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 121,
          "start": 399.74,
          "end": 402.21,
          "en": "because you are managing some state object,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 122,
          "start": 402.21,
          "end": 406.12,
          "en": "you would have to write the logic for this on your own.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 123,
          "start": 406.12,
          "end": 409.78,
          "en": "With class-based components and setState that's different,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 124,
          "start": 409.78,
          "end": 412.87,
          "en": "there react mergers this for you.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 125,
          "start": 412.87,
          "end": 416.02,
          "en": "Now here, of course I don't always wanna set this to false,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 126,
          "start": 416.02,
          "end": 419.01,
          "en": "I wanna set it to the opposite of what it was before",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 127,
          "start": 419.01,
          "end": 420.89,
          "en": "and therefore here setState",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 128,
          "start": 420.89,
          "end": 423.81,
          "en": "just like the state updating function from useState",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 129,
          "start": 424.67,
          "end": 429.63,
          "en": "also supports such a updating function.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 130,
          "start": 429.63,
          "end": 433.48,
          "en": "So here we can also pass a function to setState",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 131,
          "start": 433.48,
          "end": 436.72,
          "en": "alternatively to the object with the new state.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 132,
          "start": 436.72,
          "end": 438.26,
          "en": "And we should do that",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 133,
          "start": 438.26,
          "end": 442.42,
          "en": "if our new state depends on the previous state.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 134,
          "start": 442.42,
          "end": 444.04,
          "en": "Nonetheless, it's not enough",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 135,
          "start": 444.04,
          "end": 447.09,
          "en": "to just return a new state value like this,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 136,
          "start": 447.09,
          "end": 450.11,
